## Original task

Exercise 147 asks to draw the graphs of:

- (a) y = -5/x.
- (b) xy = 7.5.

The selected exercise has figure_ids = []; it has no source image dependency.

## Independent derivation

For (a), x cannot be zero. Multiplying by nonzero x gives xy = -5,
so x and y have opposite signs and y cannot be zero. The curve therefore
has a branch in quadrant II and a branch in quadrant IV, never in I or III.
Useful exact points are (-5, 1), (-2.5, 2), (-1, 5), (1, -5),
(2.5, -2), and (5, -1). These also prevent reversing the axes or the sign.

As x approaches 0 from the positive side, y tends to negative infinity;
from the negative side, y tends to positive infinity. As the magnitude
of x increases without bound, y approaches 0 but never equals 0.
Thus the x-axis and y-axis are asymptotes, not parts of the graph,
and the curve has no axis intercepts or point at the origin.
It increases on each separate interval x < 0 and x > 0. The two
branches are not connected through the origin or across x = 0.

For (b), xy = 7.5 rules out x = 0 and is equivalently y = 7.5/x.
Here x and y have the same sign, with neither coordinate zero.
Its branches lie in quadrants I and III. Useful exact points are
(-7.5, -1), (-3, -2.5), (-1, -7.5), (1, 7.5), (3, 2.5), and (7.5, 1).

As x approaches 0 from the positive side, y tends to positive infinity;
from the negative side, y tends to negative infinity. As the magnitude
of x increases without bound, y approaches 0 without reaching it.
Both coordinate axes are asymptotes; there are no axis intercepts.
It decreases on each separate interval x < 0 and x > 0.

Both graphs are reciprocal rectangular hyperbolas with two smooth,
disconnected branches, symmetric under (x, y) -> (-x, -y).
The sign of the constant controls their quadrant placement.
Replacing either by a straight line, treating 7.5 as a slope, or joining
points through x = 0 would not solve the original drawing task.

## Review implications fixed before candidate disclosure

A choice adaptation should retain the distinction between the two equations,
the conversion xy = 7.5 to a reciprocal relation, quadrant/sign reasoning,
and nonzero-coordinate/asymptotic branch shape.
Recognition of described graphs cannot establish a learner's actual
point plotting, scale choice, smooth-curve drawing, or construction accuracy;
those production losses must be honestly recorded.
Whether a particular recognition question retains enough mathematical
judgment is undecided until its learner-facing view is read.
